1 OCCU Huslærer Sjelland, Munkvoll, Sokneprest Alta og Talvik 1791, Veøy 1788 prost Romsdal, eidsvollsmann
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Jens Stub''', Norwegian priest and politician.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of district stipendiary magistrate (sorenskriver) [http://www.geni.com/people/Johan-Daniel-Stub/6000000002870607224 Johan Daniel Stub] (1736–1802) and grandson of [http://www.geni.com/people/Lars-Lauritz-Stub/600000
2 CONC 0007038272459 Lauritz Stub] (1708-1774), both of whom served as judges in Bergen. His 5th great grandfather was His Excellency [https://www.geni.com/people/Niels-Stub/6000000007245446604 Nils Stub] (?-1580), the  Chancellor of Nor
2 CONC way. Descendants of Nils Stub are considered noble through male-line at birth by rank nobility (rangadel) in the Kingdom of Denmark-Norway and the Duchy of Schleswig and Holstein. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He grew up in [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eid,_Norway Eid] in the county of [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sogn_og_Fjordane Sogn og Fjordane], [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norway Norway]. His brother was [http://www.geni.com/peo
2 CONC ple/Gerhard-Heiberg-Stub/6000000005440902397 Gerhard Heiberg Stub] (1781-1831), a merchant in [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bergen Bergen]. In August 1793 he married his cousin [http://www.geni.com/people/Gjertrud-Helene-Stub/6000
2 CONC 000005440887214 Gjertrud Helene Heiberg] (1774–1852) in [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talvik,_Norway Talvik].
2 CONT
2 CONT Jens Stub was a priest by education. He earned his degree in theology (Cand.theol.) in 1788. He was a Church of Norway priest in Alta to 1801 he was vicar on the island of [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ve%C3%B8y Veøy] in Romsdalsf
2 CONC jord.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stub was a member of the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norwegian_Constitutional_Assembly Norwegian Constitutional Assembly] at [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eidsvoll Eidsvoll] in 1814 at which time he worked as dean in [http://en.
2 CONC wikipedia.org/wiki/Romsdal Romsdals] Amt. He died in a boat accident in 1819 in [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tresfjord Tresfjord].
2 CONT
